WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.830
Hi guys, my name is Heinrich

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:05.270
and welcome to another coffee
break with Firm Learning.

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:07.210
I hope you're all having a good time.

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:10.770
Today I'm talking about
how you can actually

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:14.478
bring skills to your
employer, to the workplace,

00:00:14.478 --> 00:00:17.020
even as a new business joiner.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:20.250
Even if you're just
freshly out of university.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.230
And why is all of this
even relevant at all?

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:25.450
Well the truth is that usually

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:27.400
if you start at a new place

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.210
you are at a big disadvantage

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:30.820
in many areas of your work.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.420
If you work in IT you first need

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.080
to learn the system architecture

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.260
that you're working with.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:36.500
You need to learn the stack

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.110
that the company is using.

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:39.910
It will take you quite some time

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:42.150
until you're up to speed
and you will usually

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:43.980
be at a big disadvantage

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.960
compared to all the other professionals

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.210
working in your field.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:49.090
Pretty similar if you work in law.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:50.240
If you're a lawyer.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.356
You may come from
university where you learnt

00:00:52.356 --> 00:00:54.690
lots of theoretical knowledge

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.800
but then being in a law firm.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.360
Working on, for instance corporate law,

00:00:58.360 --> 00:00:59.239
getting all the things done,

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:02.100
knowing how all the things
are actually done in practice

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.330
will take you quite some time

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.160
maybe years to learn
until you're at a level

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.870
where you're on par with other
professionals in your field.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:12.060
And of course, if you're a
normal business practitioner

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.110
this is very similar as well.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:15.620
You might work with certain tools,

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:16.970
with certain software.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:19.937
There's a certain
architecture that the people

00:01:19.937 --> 00:01:20.910
are working with.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.900
And it will take you quite some time

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.990
until you catch up and really understand

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:27.570
all the business processes to a level

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:29.510
that other people in the organization

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:32.030
who maybe worked there
already for several years.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:35.510
However, all these things being said,

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:38.750
from my experience after
many years in consulting

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.316
I believe that there
are two technical skills

00:01:41.316 --> 00:01:44.770
that you can learn pretty
quickly as a new joiner

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:49.340
and if you do you usually, at
least in these specific areas,

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.690
add a significant advantage
compared to other people

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:54.280
working in your organization.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.100
And these two technical skills are first,

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:59.370
PowerPoint, being good in PowerPoint

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:02.070
and second, Excel, being
good at Microsoft Excel.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:03.730
So let's talk about these two.

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:05.050
Why are they relevant?

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:07.290
Why do I believe that this can really

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.160
make a difference for
your professional life?

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:10.880
And what can you actually do

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.690
to build this skillset
and improve your skills?

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:15.310
Let's start with PowerPoint.

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:17.510
The truth is that pretty much

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:19.530
all larger corporations

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.500
and also most of the SMEs,

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.620
the small and medium-sized enterprises

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.750
use PowerPoint on a daily basis

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:29.150
in almost all of their business functions.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:30.910
And of course on the one-hand side

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:32.900
this is for internal presentations,

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.830
for internal communication

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.560
but then on the other side
also from communication

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.440
for external stakeholders like customers,

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.040
like banks or other investors

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.660
or any other stakeholder that might be.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.200
And you can imagine especially
for this latter type

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.060
of presentations it is super crucial

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:56.230
and pretty much always
in the highest interest

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.970
of the C level executives in your company

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:01.280
that these presentations are getting done

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.360
at the highest professional level

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.610
and they're meeting really
the highest out there.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:08.120
If you imagine that usually
the sea of offerances

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.480
need to present something
in front of investors,

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:11.640
in front of banks,

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.120
he wants to make sure
that the things he present

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:16.890
not only in the terms of the
content that he's showing

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.770
but also the style, the formal appearance

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:22.090
of the presentation is as professional

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:23.160
as it can be.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.330
Similar to anything relating
to sales presentations

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:27.430
in front of customers

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:29.921
or other things that might happen,

00:03:29.921 --> 00:03:30.810
other stakeholders that
might be out there.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.660
And the other truth is
that in a corporation

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:35.680
from my experience having worked

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:37.530
with many German corporations

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:40.210
but also other corporations
throughout Europe

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:41.907
and also in other continents,

00:03:41.907 --> 00:03:45.640
very, very few people in an organization,

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.430
even in a large blue chip company

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:51.001
have the skill to create
PowerPoint presentations

00:03:51.001 --> 00:03:53.380
in a way that this really meets

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:55.830
this highest of standards.

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:58.950
Yes it's true that many
people have some basic skills

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:00.610
and can create some slides,

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:03.103
but these slides are not at a level

00:04:03.103 --> 00:04:04.410
that you would expect them

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:07.380
if for instance you work
in a large consulting firm

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.650
like McKinsey or BCG where
these are really skills

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.670
that are valued and skills are developed

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:15.100
and honed over time.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:17.300
So what does this mean for you?

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:20.800
If you can bring these
PowerPoint skills to the table,

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:24.500
if you can get to a level
where you are better

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:27.950
in creating PowerPoint pages
than almost all other people

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:28.912
in the organization,

00:04:28.912 --> 00:04:31.670
this can be a huge advantage for you

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:34.190
up to the point that even the CEO,

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:37.110
the C level executives of your company

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:38.650
want to work together with you

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:40.030
even as a junior colleague

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:41.720
because you can help them

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.480
to get these presentations

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.730
to a level where it's really making

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:47.500
a difference for these people.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:48.960
And if you are at the point

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.810
you suddenly have exposure

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:54.190
to the most important,
most senior projects

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:57.240
that your specific employer is working on

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:00.710
and you have direct exposure
to these C level executives

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:04.110
where other people might
need years and years

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:06.730
until they are at a point
where this might be possible.

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:08.210
And again, from my experience

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:10.060
from what I have experienced

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:12.970
it is not that difficult to get there.

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:15.600
I've seen junior colleagues
learn these skills

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.420
in a quite short amount of time

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:19.360
and then being sought after

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.090
by these senior people in organization.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:23.680
So what can you actually do

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:26.320
in order to improve your
skills in PowerPoint?

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:28.630
Well of course one point is always

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:29.560
learning by doing.

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:33.180
The more presentations you
do, the better you will get.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.639
But then the other point is also

00:05:35.639 --> 00:05:37.000
that you need some kind of guidance.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.320
You need to know how presentations

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:40.810
in front of senior investors,

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:43.360
senior customers on board levels,

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:46.500
how these presentations
actually need to look like

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.240
because if you have nobody
giving you that coaching

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:50.720
that you get in consulting,

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:52.110
you just cannot get there, right?

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:54.160
Because you have no
guidance in how to do that.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.217
And of course you can ask other people

00:05:57.217 --> 00:05:58.660
who have done that.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:01.250
You can look at
presentations if you get some

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:03.260
of that senior level in your company

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:05.956
where you see how these
are supposed to look like

00:06:05.956 --> 00:06:08.330
and of course the value that I can bring

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:11.060
is in this channel that if
you look into the play lists

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:12.940
if you look in prior videos

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:16.310
I will link a few of them here above,

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:18.320
I'm talking about these skills.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:20.500
I'm actually teaching
in several of my videos

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:22.810
what you need to do in order
to get your presentations

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:23.643
at that level.

00:06:23.643 --> 00:06:25.680
So feel free to look at these videos

